\image(figures/gabow_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The frame for vertex1 is gone when the later edge 2→1 is examined. Vertex 1 is still in S with no component ID. Popping candidate2 changes B while leaving S and the active frames in place.)
\alttext(Three native boxes show the actual state after DFS 1 finishes, before edge 2 to1 is processed, and after candidate2 is popped. S grows to three vertices while the active DFS has only two frames.)

The trace's edge event occurs after advancing the current neighbor index and
before acting on the target. A collapse event follows one boundary pop.
An assignment event follows one S pop and component write; component-counter
increment follows the last assignment of that component. These primitive
snapshots expose intermediate states, so not every boundary invariant is
intended to hold halfway through a closure. A target field of -1 is unused for
enter, close-root and finish events.

> Why the boundaries identify strong components

Assign discovery numbers pre(v) in DFS visit order. Every newly discovered
vertex is pushed onto S and B. If an examined target v is already discovered
and unassigned, remove candidate roots while

$$
\operatorname{pre}(\operatorname{top}(B))>\operatorname{pre}(v).
$$
\alttext(Pop candidate roots whose discovery number is greater than the examined open target's number.)

The boundary candidates mark consecutive blocks of open vertices. Such blocks
represent vertices already contracted together by discovered return paths.
They form a path in the contracted graph, rather than necessarily the active
DFS path in the original graph. An edge to an open earlier block joins a cycle
of those contracted blocks, so intervening boundaries disappear.
An edge to an assigned component cannot reopen it.

When u finishes its adjacency and remains the top candidate, pop its boundary
and assign the suffix of S through u as one component. All its outgoing
possibilities have been processed, so this is the next sink of the remaining
quotient graph. Continuing the search finds every vertex's maximal mutually
reachable class. This does not enumerate individual cycles.

Gabow's [2000 paper, Path-based depth-first search for strong and biconnected components](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0020-0190(00)00051-X),
Section 2 and Theorem 2.1, printed pages 108–110, develops this contraction view
and the stack implementation
([full journal paper](https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/spr03/cs423/download/path_based_dfs.pdf)).
The native code already used an explicit heap DFS. It stores candidate vertex
IDs with monotonic preorder values and a separate component array; the paper
uses stack-index boundaries and a combined index/component array.

Each vertex enters and leaves each stack once. Each adjacency entry is examined
once. A single edge may pop many boundaries, but at most N boundaries are popped
over the whole run. Thus traversal takes O(N+E) time with O(N) auxiliary state,
excluding the given adjacency and returned labels. This is an amortized argument;
the original report's “one comparison per edge” claim was too strong.
Captured full-state history has additional costs, discussed below.

> Preserve the original eight-vertex graph

The native fixture has cycles 0↔1,2→3→4→2,5↔6 and the sink 7, linked by 1→2,4→5,6→7.
Raw component IDs close in order 0,1,2,3 and therefore run opposite the graph's
source-to-sink direction. Every cross-component arc u→v satisfies id[u]>id[v].
That strict decrease also proves that the quotient is acyclic.
The quotient's dense row indices follow ascending raw labels; raw labels and
dense quotient indices are conceptually distinct.

The summary helper groups vertices and sorts/deduplicates quotient arcs.
Its map and sort work is separate from the linear SCC kernel.
It summarizes supplied labels; it does not independently prove that an arbitrary
labeling is a valid SCC decomposition.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gabow_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/gabow_scc.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/graph_connectivity.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/depth_first_search.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/brute_force_search.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto graph=dm::algorithms::gabow_scc_example_graph();
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::gabow_scc_trace(8,graph);
  const auto kosaraju=dm::algorithms::kosaraju_scc(8,graph);
  const auto q=dm::algorithms::scc_summary(graph,t.components);
  const bool agrees=dm::algorithms::scc_same_partition(t.components,kosaraju);
  std::print("Original labels {}; components {}; Kosaraju agrees {}; raw internal edges {}; raw cross edges {}; unique quotient arcs {}; reverse topological {}.\n",t.components,q.groups.size(),agrees,q.internal_edges,q.cross_edges,q.unique_cross_edges,q.reverse_topological);
  std::print("\n| raw component ID | original vertices | size | quotient neighbor indices |\n| ---: | --- | ---: | --- |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<q.groups.size();++i)std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",q.groups[i].label,q.groups[i].vertices,q.groups[i].vertices.size(),q.adjacency[i]);
  const auto& w=t.work;
  std::print("\nActual work: {} visits; {} examined edges; tree {}; open {}; closed {}; boundary collapses {}; closures {}; events {}; retained entries {}.\n",w.visited_vertices,w.edges_examined,w.tree_edges,w.open_edges,w.closed_edges,w.root_collapses,w.component_closures,w.event_count,t.captured_entries);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(58,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=3.2;node.height=1.7;node.font_size=22;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<q.groups.size();++i){const auto& g=q.groups[i];d.node(std::to_string(i),{4.8*double(q.groups.size()-1-i),0},std::format("raw ID {}\nvertices {}\nsize {}",g.label,g.vertices,g.vertices.size()),node);}
  for(std::size_t u=0;u<q.adjacency.size();++u)for(int v:q.adjacency[u])d.edge(std::to_string(u),std::to_string(v),"");
  d.text({7.2,2.6},"Actual original quotient: IDs decrease along every arrow",26)
   .text({7.2,-2.2},"The four groups form a chain; each quotient arc occurs once",24)
   .save_svg("build/gabow_original.svg");
  if(!agrees||t.components!=std::vector<int>{3,3,2,2,2,1,1,0}||!q.reverse_topological)throw std::runtime_error("original SCC partition");
}
```
