> Whole-request history admission and complete validation

Ordinary and work-only calls keep the current state. A trace owns the original
rows and a complete copy of comp, preorder, S, B and active frames at each actual
transition. It can therefore require O((N+E)N) copying and retained storage.
The history quota counts entries, not bytes or peak RAM.

Input rows and neighbor entries cost N+E. Each snapshot costs one metadata record,
two full N-entry arrays and up to N records in each of S, B and the frame vector.
A frame pair counts as one record. The conservative whole-request bound is

$$
(N+E)+(E+4N)(1+5N).
$$
\alttext(Whole trace entries are input rows plus neighbors, then the transition count times one metadata record plus at most five arrays of N entries.)

The default cap is one million entries. Zero disables only this optional cap;
count and container checks remain. Full graph validation precedes admission,
and admission precedes graph copies and traversal-state allocation.
There is no early-stop discount, truncation or partial trace. The quota excludes
ordinary final labels, work/scalar root metadata, working state and allocator
bytes. It is not a whole-process memory limit.

Negative vertex counts, wrong row counts and every out-of-range endpoint are
rejected, even if a tiny quota or empty shortcut would otherwise terminate.
The empty graph is represented by n=0 with no rows. Loops, duplicates and neighbor
order are valid input and are retained.

The tiny graph needs a whole upper bound of 263 entries, although its actual
snapshots retain 185. A cap of 185 still rejects it; cap 263 admits it. The deep
cycle below uses 200,000 explicit heap frames successfully in work-only mode;
its full snapshot history is rejected before traversal under the default quota.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gabow_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/gabow_scc.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/graph_connectivity.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/depth_first_search.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/brute_force_search.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::algorithms::SccAdjacency graph{{1,2},{0},{1}};
  bool rejected=false;try{(void)dm::algorithms::gabow_scc_trace(3,graph,185);}catch(const std::length_error&){rejected=true;}
  auto t=dm::algorithms::gabow_scc_trace(3,graph,263);
  graph[0].clear();t.events[0].path[0]=99;
  const bool owned=t.adjacency[0]==std::vector<int>{1,2}&&t.events[1].path[0]==0&&t.components[0]==0;
  bool short_rows=false,negative_edge=false,high_edge=false,overflow=false;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::gabow_scc_trace(2,{{}},1);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){short_rows=true;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::gabow_scc_trace(1,{{-1}},1);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){negative_edge=true;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::gabow_scc_trace(1,{{1}},1);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){high_edge=true;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::gabow_scc_trace_entries(1,std::numeric_limits<std::size_t>::max());}catch(const std::length_error&){overflow=true;}
  const auto empty=dm::algorithms::gabow_scc_trace(0,{});
  std::print("Tiny whole bound {}; actual retained {}; cap 185 rejected {}; cap 263 admitted true; owned after caller/event edits {}. Original bound {}.\n",dm::algorithms::gabow_scc_trace_entries(3,4),t.captured_entries,rejected,owned,dm::algorithms::gabow_scc_trace_entries(8,10));
  std::print("Validation: short rows rejected {}; negative endpoint rejected {}; high endpoint rejected {}; virtual overflow rejected {}; empty labels {}; empty events {}.\n",short_rows,negative_edge,high_edge,overflow,empty.components.size(),empty.events.size());
  constexpr int n=200000;dm::algorithms::SccAdjacency cycle(n);
  for(auto [u,v]:dm::algorithms::rooted_tree_edges(std::size_t(n),1))cycle[u].push_back(int(v));cycle.back().push_back(0);
  const auto deep=dm::algorithms::gabow_scc_work(n,cycle);bool denied=false;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::gabow_scc_trace(n,cycle);}catch(const std::length_error&){denied=true;}
  std::print("Deep cycle: vertices {}; peak DFS {}; peak open {}; collapses {}; components {}; transitions {}; default history quota rejected {}.\n",deep.work.visited_vertices,deep.work.peak_dfs,deep.work.peak_open,deep.work.root_collapses,deep.work.component_closures,deep.work.event_count,denied);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot({263.,double(t.captured_entries),185.},{"Whole upper bound","Actual retained","Rejected cap"});
  plot.title("Whole-request admission uses 263, despite actual 185").xlab("Tiny graph with N=3,E=4").ylab("Retained-history entries; not bytes").y_limits(0,290).size(1050,520).save_svg("build/gabow_controls.svg");
  if(!rejected||!owned||!short_rows||!negative_edge||!high_edge||!overflow||!denied||deep.work.peak_dfs!=std::size_t(n))throw std::runtime_error("admission and graph domains");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Tiny whole bound 263; actual retained 185; cap 185 rejected true; cap 263 admitted true; owned after caller/event edits true. Original bound 1740.
Validation: short rows rejected true; negative endpoint rejected true; high endpoint rejected true; virtual overflow rejected true; empty labels 0; empty events 0.
Deep cycle: vertices 200000; peak DFS 200000; peak open 200000; collapses 199999; components 1; transitions 1000000; default history quota rejected true.
\image(figures/gabow_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The whole bound remains conservative even though the number of events is known exactly. A fitting actual retained count does not bypass admission; validation still covers every row and endpoint before the quota.)
\alttext(Three bars show the whole upper bound263, actual retained185 and rejected cap 185 for the tiny graph. The output also verifies a 200,000-vertex work-only cycle.)

The SCC kernel, work counters and snapshots share one traversal. Raw label
preservation supports reproducibility; partition equality expresses the
graph-theoretic result. Quotient summaries and diagrams make that result
readable, while independent reachability checks establish what agreement
between two SCC implementations alone cannot prove.
